/// A thin, transparent, zero-cost newtype over an `Int` errno value.
///
/// `Errno` is the error arm of the libc `*_bytes` syscall wrappers —
/// `Result[Bytes, Errno]` (read/recv/getentropy) and `Result[Int, Errno]`
/// (write/send). It is nominally distinct from `Int` so that, for a wrapper
/// like `write_bytes -> Result[Int, Errno]`, the count-written (`Int`) and the
/// errno can never be confused — the exact confusion a raw `Result[Int, Int]`
/// would invite.
///
/// **Transparent / zero-cost (codegen invariant).** `Errno` carries no box or
/// tag over the `Int` on the syscall return path: in emitted C, a `Result`
/// whose error arm is `Errno` holds a bare `int64_t err;`, never a wrapper
/// struct. Constructing `Err(Errno(rc))` stores `rc` directly; matching
/// `Err(Errno(rc))` reads it back by identity.
///
/// Domain-neutral by design (a file write returning a network error type would
/// be a semantic lie). No methods in v1 — extract the code by matching
/// `Errno(rc)`.
pub type Errno {
    Errno(Int)
}
